-# Horizon
+## Start here
 
-**Minecraft: Java Edition 26.3, rewritten in Rust.** Your worlds, the same rules and the same terrain as the Java game, checked against it by hundreds of differential tests, in one native program with no Java. Free, open source (MIT) and unofficial: you play it with your own copy of Java Edition.
+| I want to… | Go here |
+| --- | --- |
+| Build and install the Chromebook app | [Chromebook guide](web/iwa/README.md) |
+| Build a normal browser version | [Web build guide](web/README.md#build-and-run) |
+| Understand the browser port's limits | [Known web limitations](web/README.md#whats-missing) |
+| Work on the engine | [Architecture](docs/architecture.md) |
 
-> **This is a fork with a browser port.** It is based on [horizon-rust/horizon](https://github.com/horizon-rust/horizon) and adds an experimental build of the client for the web: WebAssembly drawing through WebGPU, with threads as Web Workers. Everything else below is upstream's README. The port is described in [Horizon in the browser](#horizon-in-the-browser), and the badges, release links and launcher downloads point at upstream.
+**Status:** IWA packaging and compatibility changes are experimental. Packaging
+checks use fixtures; a complete game launch inside ChromeOS still needs testing.
+No playable download is included. Building needs resources prepared from your
+own Minecraft installation; do not commit or publicly upload those resources.
 
